\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f0\fs24 180\plain \f0\super\fs32 v\plain \f0\fs24  {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezwrk[1].6930gi/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f0\scaps\fs24\chcbpat17 Commentarius in Aristotelem De Anima}}\plain \f0\fs24 . Dieselbe Hand wie 171\plain \f0\super\fs32 r\plain \f0\fs24 . Notizen, rechts-, links- und kopfst\u228 \'e4ndig. Beginnt \plain \f0\i\fs24 ipsum non contingere movere alterum, eorum que moventur \plain \f0\i\ul\fs24 ad\plain \f0\i\fs24  animam (...) \plain \f0\i\ul\fs24 arbitrati sunt\plain \f0\i\fs24  esse. Unde Democritus quidem ignem quendam ... - ... expedit (terrore suprascripta) coniux. \plain \f0\fs24 Bezug: {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[1].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f0\scaps\fs24\chcbpat17 Aristoteles}}\plain \f0\scaps\fs24 : {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezwrk[1].6930/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f0\scaps\fs24\chcbpat17 De anima}}\plain \f0\fs24  403b bis 404a. Wohl nicht von Rufus. Zu mittelalterlichen Auslegungen der aristotelischen Seelenlehre vgl. Richard C. Dales, The Problem of the Rational Soul in the Thirteenth Century (Brill's Studies in Intellectual History 65), Leiden, New York, K\u246 \'f6ln 1995, S. 203-205. }

